---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import GuideList from "../../components/GuideList.astro";
import Simple from "../../layouts/Simple.astro";
import { visitIdsForArticle } from "../../data/catalog";

const articles = (await getCollection("articles", ({ data }) => data.section === "alternatives")).sort(
  (a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order,
);
const description =
  "Analytics alternatives for founders leaving GA4, PostHog, Mixpanel, Plausible, Fathom, or Amplitude. See who should switch, who should stay, and the price.";
---
<Simple
  title="Analytics Alternatives"
  description={description}
  kicker="Alternatives"
  lede="Switch when the tool you have is not answering the question you open every week. Stay when it already does. DataFast fits revenue by channel. It does not fit a product analytics suite."
  crumbs={[{ href: "/", label: "Home" }, { label: "Alternatives" }]}
>
  <p>People usually want one of three things: a lighter report than GA4, a privacy-first traffic script, or a product suite that can stand in for Amplitude, Mixpanel, or PostHog. Those are different shortlists.</p>
  <p>DataFast belongs on the first two when the question is which channel or page brought a payment. PostHog, Mixpanel, and Amplitude belong on the third. Plausible and Fathom belong with privacy-first traffic, and their revenue features are real without being the whole product. Public prices are as of 26 November 2026.</p>
  <p>If the tool you have today is DataFast, start with the <a href="/alternatives/datafast/">DataFast alternatives</a>. That page covers when to stay, then links the six <a href="/compare/">comparisons</a> and the <a href="/pricing/datafast/">pricing ladder</a>. The <a href="/reviews/datafast/">DataFast review</a> covers the product. The <a href="/compare/posthog-vs-google-analytics/">PostHog vs GA4 comparison</a> is the matchup for teams choosing between those two.</p>
  <section slot="wide" class="band band-yellow">
    <div class="article-wide">
      <h2>Analytics alternatives</h2>
      <GuideList
        items={articles.map((article) => ({
          href: `/${article.id}/`,
          title: article.data.title,
          description: article.data.description,
          visits: visitIdsForArticle(article.id),
        }))}
      />
    </div>
  </section>
</Simple>
